Lab Material
Lab 15: Implementing API Gateway with Camel
Objectives
By the end of this lab, you will be able to:

Understand the API Gateway pattern and its role in microservices architecture
Implement API mediation routes using Apache Camel
Configure security mechanisms including authentication and rate limiting
Aggregate responses from multiple backend services
Test and interpret API Gateway performance under load
Identify production considerations for deploying an API Gateway
Prerequisites
Before starting this lab, you should have:

Basic understanding of REST APIs and HTTP protocols
Familiarity with Java programming language
Knowledge of Maven build tool
Understanding of microservices architecture concepts
Basic Linux command line skills
Familiarity with JSON data formats
Your practice environment should have the following tools installed. Verify with these commands before starting:

java -version
mvn -version
curl --version
If jq is not installed (used later for pretty-printing JSON), install it with:

sudo apt-get update && sudo apt-get install -y jq
This lab uses Java 11, Maven 3.8+, Apache Camel 3.20, and Spring Boot 2.7. All dependencies are pulled automatically by Maven, so no manual downloads are required.

Lab Environment Setup
Work in an isolated, self-owned practice environment (a personal VM or sandbox). This lab builds a local API Gateway that proxies to a public test API (jsonplaceholder.typicode.com) so you can observe real request mediation, authentication, rate limiting, and aggregation behavior without needing your own backend services.

