Task 3: Test and Interpret Gateway Performance
Subtask 3.1: Create the Performance Testing Script
Create a script that measures latency, tests concurrent load, and checks rate limiting and large-payload handling:

cat > performance_test.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash

AUTH_HEADER="Authorization: Basic $(echo -n 'admin:password' | base64)"
BASE_URL="http://localhost:8080/api/v1"

echo "=== API Gateway Performance Testing ==="

measure_response_time() {
    local url=$1
    local method=${2:-GET}
    local data=${3:-""}

    if [ "$method" = "POST" ] && [ -n "$data" ]; then
        curl -s -w "%{time_total},%{http_code},%{size_download}\n" \
             -o /dev/null \
             -X "$method" \
             -H "$AUTH_HEADER" \
             -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
             -d "$data" \
             "$url"
    else
        curl -s -w "%{time_total},%{http_code},%{size_download}\n" \
             -o /dev/null \
             -X "$method" \
             -H "$AUTH_HEADER" \
             "$url"
    fi
}

echo -e "\n1. Single Request Latency Test"
echo "URL,Method,Response_Time(s),HTTP_Code,Size(bytes)"

endpoints=(
    "$BASE_URL/health,GET"
    "$BASE_URL/users,GET"
    "$BASE_URL/users/1,GET"
    "$BASE_URL/products,GET"
    "$BASE_URL/products/1,GET"
)

for endpoint in "${endpoints[@]}"; do
    IFS=',' read -r url method <<< "$endpoint"
    result=$(measure_response_time "$url" "$method")
    echo "$url,$method,$result"
done

echo -e "\n2. Concurrent Requests Test (5 concurrent requests)"

concurrent_test() {
    local url="$BASE_URL/health"
    local concurrent_requests=5
    local temp_file
    temp_file=$(mktemp)

    for i in $(seq 1 $concurrent_requests); do
        (
            result=$(measure_response_time "$url")
            echo "Request_$i,$result" >> "$temp_file"
        ) &
    done

    wait

    echo "Request_ID,Response_Time(s),HTTP_Code,Size(bytes)"
    sort "$temp_file"

    awk -F',' '{
        sum += $2
        if ($2 > max || max == "") max = $2
        if ($2 < min || min == "") min = $2
        count++
    } END {
        if (count > 0) {
            avg = sum / count
            printf "\nAverage Response Time: %.3f seconds\n", avg
            printf "Min Response Time: %.3f seconds\n", min
            printf "Max Response Time: %.3f seconds\n", max
            printf "Total Requests: %d\n", count
        }
    }' "$temp_file"

    rm "$temp_file"
}

concurrent_test

echo -e "\n3. Rate Limiting Verification (12 sequential requests)"
echo "Request_Number,Response_Time(s),HTTP_Code,Size(bytes)"

for i in $(seq 1 12); do
    result=$(measure_response_time "$BASE_URL/users")
    echo "$i,$result"
done

echo -e "\n4. Large Payload Handling Test"

resource_test() {
    local url="$BASE_URL/users"
    local large_payload='{"name":"Test User","email":"test@example.com","address":{"street":"123 Test St","city":"Test City","zipcode":"12345"},"company":{"name":"Test Company","catchPhrase":"Testing is important","bs":"test business"}}'

    echo "Testing GET against $url with authenticated session under simulated load..."
    for i in $(seq 1 3); do
        result=$(measure_response_time "$url" "GET")
        echo "Large_Payload_Test_$i,$result"
    done
}

resource_test

echo -e "\n5. Error Handling and Timeout Test"

error_handling_test() {
    echo "Testing request to a non-existent route (expect 404):"
    curl -s -o /dev/null -w "HTTP Status: %{http_code}\n" "$BASE_URL/nonexistent"

    echo "Testing request with a short timeout to check gateway responsiveness:"
    curl -s -o /dev/null -w "HTTP Status: %{http_code}, Time: %{time_total}s\n" \
        --max-time 5 \
        -H "$AUTH_HEADER" \
        "$BASE_URL/health"
}

error_handling_test

echo -e "\n=== Performance Testing Complete ==="
EOF

chmod +x performance_test.sh
./performance_test.sh
Deliverable: The script runs to completion without syntax errors and prints five labeled sections. Section 3 shows the first 10 rows with HTTP 200 and the last 2 rows with HTTP 429, confirming the rate limiter is active during a performance run.

Subtask 3.2: Interpret Performance Test Results
Review the output columns: time_total (seconds spent per request), http_code, and size_download (response body size in bytes).

Look for these patterns in your results:

Compare the single-request latency for /health (no backend call) against /users (proxies to a real backend). The difference approximates the network and processing overhead added by the backend hop.
In the concurrent test, if the max response time is noticeably higher than the average, that request likely queued behind others due to limited thread capacity.
In the rate limiting section, confirm exactly which request number first receives HTTP 429. This confirms the sliding window logic in RateLimitProcessor triggers at the configured maxRequestsPerMinute value of 10.
