Troubleshooting
Issue 1: curl requests return "Connection refused" on port 8080. Diagnostic hint: Check that mvn spring-boot:run is still running in the background with jobs or ps aux | grep spring-boot, and confirm no other process is already bound to port 8080 with lsof -i :8080 (or wait longer after startup, since Spring Boot can take 15-20 seconds to initialize).

Issue 2: Every request returns HTTP 401 even with the correct Authorization header. Diagnostic hint: Confirm the base64 encoding was generated without a trailing newline (echo -n), and print the decoded value locally with echo 'YWRtaW46cGFzc3dvcmQ=' | base64 -d to verify it matches admin:password exactly.

Conclusion
In this lab, you built a functional API Gateway using Apache Camel and Spring Boot, implementing the core responsibilities of the API Gateway pattern: request routing and mediation, centralized authentication, rate limiting, and response aggregation across multiple backend calls. You verified each capability with concrete HTTP tests, then used a complete performance testing script to gather latency and concurrency data and confirm the rate limiter's behavior under load. Along the way you saw how Spring Boot Actuator provides ready-made observability into request counts and timings, and you reviewed the gaps between this lab's simplified implementation and a production-grade deployment, including clustering, centralized logging, secrets management, and circuit breaking. These are the same architectural concerns that govern real microservices platforms, where an API Gateway acts as the single controlled entry point protecting and coordinating access to many internal services. To continue learning, explore Camel's support for OAuth2 and JWT-based authentication, integrate a distributed rate-limiting backend such as Redis, and experiment with Resilience4j circuit breakers to see how the gateway behaves when a backend service becomes unresponsive.
